<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Go-Toba</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1180">1180</a>–1239</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Go-Toba was emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from 1183 to 1198 and then ruled as retired emperor. Seeking to restore imperial power, he raised forces against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jokyu-war">Jokyu War</a> of 1221 but was defeated by the Hojo regents and exiled to the Oki Islands, where he died. He was a leading poet and commissioned the Shin Kokinshu anthology.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-go-toba.jpg" alt="Emperor Go-Toba" width="320" height="325" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fujiwara no Nobuzane · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emperor_Go-Toba.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Go-Toba was the fourth son of Emperor Takakura and a grandson of Emperor Go-Shirakawa. He took the throne at the age of three, on 8 September 1183, after the child emperor Antoku had fled the capital and the cloistered former emperor Go-Shirakawa elevated Antoku's younger brother by decree. Because the imperial regalia were still held by Antoku, his accession became the first in history to omit the ritual transmission of the sacred treasures from one sovereign to the next. In 1192 Go-Shirakawa died and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Minamoto no Yoritomo</a> established the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>, and the emperor became a figurehead. In 1198 Go-Toba abdicated in favour of his son, Emperor Tsuchimikado.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Abdication freed Go-Toba, who was eighteen, from what has been called the ceremonial prison of the imperial palace, and most of his activity as calligrapher, painter, musician, poet, critic and editor belongs to the years after it. He took pleasure in archery, horsemanship and swordsmanship, summoned swordsmiths to court, gave them honorary titles and learned their craft; his patronage is credited with beginning a golden age of Japanese bladesmithing.</p><p>His main contribution to literature was the Shin Kokinshu, the New Anthology of Ancient and Modern Waka, which he ordered and for which he revived the Office of Waka as headquarters and served as an editor. He also held many poetry parties and competitions. Fujiwara no Teika chose one of his poems as number 99 of the Hyakunin Isshu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>As cloistered emperor from 1198 to 1221, during the reigns of three emperors, Go-Toba held less power than earlier cloistered emperors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heian-period">Heian period</a>. During this time he decreed that the followers of the Pure Land sect in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honen">Honen</a>, be banished or in some cases executed. In 1221 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> installed his three-year-old grandson as Emperor Chukyo, and Go-Toba rebelled in an attempt to reclaim the throne and overthrow the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> shogunate, a conflict known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jokyu-war">Jokyu War</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">Samurai</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> supported him, but most samurai, particularly in the Kanto, backed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>, encouraged by Hojo Masako, the widow of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Yoritomo</a>. The rebellion failed, Chukyo was replaced by Go-Horikawa, and Go-Toba was exiled to the Oki Islands. There he went on composing waka and prepared a private edition of the Shin Kokinshu, the Oki edition, dropping about 400 poems; he died and was buried on the islands.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emperor Go-Toba” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They defeated the retired emperor <b>Go-Toba</b> in the Jokyu War of 1221 and issued the Goseibai shikimoku law code in 1232 for warriors.</blockquote><a href="/state/kamakura-shogunate">Kamakura shogunate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kyoto, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Nakanoshima. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.01,135.77&amp;year=1218" aria-label="Kyoto on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7991 897 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Emperor Go-Toba"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8441 1167h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8441 1167h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.01,135.77&amp;year=1218">What was here in 1218? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
